          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (911 in the US, or your local emergency number). Do not wait.** Ask someone near you to call, or call yourself if you can.\n\nBeing able to say only a few words before stopping for breath is a warning sign. The Asthma reference in this corpus says that difficulty speaking in full sentences, or breathlessness at rest, needs immediate emergency attention. The severe breathing guidance adds that a rescue inhaler that is not working is a reason to get urgent help, and that a quiet chest during a bad attack means the airways have narrowed further, not that things are settling.\n\nWhile you wait for help, stay sitting upright and try to stay calm. Do not be alone if you can avoid it. Tell the operator that your breathing is very difficult and your rescue inhaler is not helping.\n\nThe tool I checked did not flag this as an emergency. That result does not change the advice above. The passages I found do not say what to do about your inhaler tonight, so please get emergency care rather than waiting for more information.\n\n*Sources: Severe difficulty breathing (emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance); Asthma (general clinical reference).*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
